# Overview
#
# A signpost is the writing-guide term for a phrase that announces what
# the text is about to do instead of doing it (`Let's dive in`, `In this
# section, we'll explore the scheduler`). The reader reads the
# announcement and learns nothing about the subject. The fix deletes the
# announcement and opens on the first point. The rule sits at
# Recommended, because `walks you through` also matches when a program
# is the guide.
#
# Expression
#
# `main` spells out each `let's` and `we'll` phrase with its verb
# (`let's explore`, `we'll cover`), and it matches `walk you through`,
# `walks you through`, and `walking you through`.
#
# `in this` before `section`, `guide`, `article`, `document`, `post`,
# `tutorial`, or `chapter` counts only with a comma glued to the noun
# and the subject of a clause after the comma, the form that a sentence
# opener takes. A clause is a subject and its verb, such as `the tests
# pass`. Harper's part-of-speech tagger marks each word with a tag for
# its word class, and a pattern names a tag in capitals. The subject
# carries the tag for a determiner (`DET`), a pronoun (`PRON`), a proper
# noun (`PROPN`), or a noun (`NOUN`). A determiner is a word that
# introduces a noun, such as `the`, `a`, or `this`. A proper noun is a
# name that takes a capital letter, such as `Kubernetes`. By default, a
# Weir match stops at a comma, so `let scope "Sentence"` lets the `in
# this` branch cross one.
#
# False Positives
#
# The bare verbs after `let's` and `we'll` also narrate code (`we'll
# take the lock`, `let's try the fast path`), so only the spelled-out
# phrases count.
#
# Where the comma or the subject is missing, `in this section` locates a
# setting (`the keys in this section of the config`) or ends a clause
# (`the features we cover in this chapter, so see the reference`), so it
# passes.
#
# `walks you through` still matches when a program guides the user (`the
# wizard walks you through the setup`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Required ProseFiller holds the signposts that point at what the
# reader can see (`as you can see`).
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 251 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled
# hits announced what the text was about to do or had done. The Rust
# book holds 228 of the 251 lines.
expr main [(let's dive), (let's explore), (let's take a look), (let's take a closer look), (let's look at), (let's break down), (let's break it down), (let's walk through), (let's unpack), (let's examine), (let's recap), (let's review), (let's get started), (let's begin), (let's start by), (let's see how), (let's see what), (let's jump in), (let's jump into), (we'll explore), (we will explore), (we'll cover), (we will cover), (we'll walk through), (we will walk through), (we'll dive), (we'll take a look), (we'll look at), (we will look at), (we'll discuss), (we will discuss), (we'll examine), (we will examine), (in this [section, guide, article, document, post, tutorial, chapter], [DET, PRON, PROPN, NOUN]), ([walk, walks, walking] you through)]
let message "Signpost. Delete the announcement and open on the first point."
let description "Flags phrases that announce what the text is about to do, such as `let's dive in` or `in this section`."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Let's Encrypt issues the certificate."
allows "The keys in this section of the config are optional."
allows "If the lock is free, we'll take it."
allows "Cargo does more than the features we cover in this chapter, so see the reference."
